# Alpha Metallurgical IR PDFs: image-heavy renderings of filings already held as
# SEC EDGAR HTML (AMR/investor-documents/sec-edgar/). 71-185 MB each at ~200 KB
# per page versus ~17 KB for the clean copy; the four largest exceed GitHub's
# 100 MB hard limit. The pipeline reads research/evidence/_text/, not these.
AMR/investor-documents/ir-amr/987512b3-9ad8-4b20-b999-8c93f744bb1b.pdf
AMR/investor-documents/ir-amr/a0385b14-49ec-446b-8996-3ad09f364239.pdf
AMR/investor-documents/ir-amr/b7c2a924-c78a-41f9-b57e-bbaae65ab7c8.pdf
AMR/investor-documents/ir-amr/AMR-022624-10-K.pdf
AMR/investor-documents/ir-amr/5ef6ee56-35a3-4634-b006-88943c834e39.pdf

# Monolithic dashboard payloads: BUILD INTERMEDIATES, not artifacts.
# dashboard_data.json (76MB) and insights.json (73MB) are rewritten on every
# build and duplicate data already committed as core.json + the per-ticker and
# per-section shards, which are what the SPA actually loads. Committing them
# added ~149MB per rebuild and is most of why .git reached 19GB.
#
# They are still WRITTEN and still read within a single build run. What made
# untracking safe is that the anti-clobber guards no longer depend on the
# committed copies: build_dashboard_data.load_prior_rows() and
# build_insights.load_prior_insights() reconstruct their baseline from the
# committed shards when the monolith is absent. Without those fallbacks,
# deleting these files SILENTLY DISABLES every sparse-rebuild guard, because a
# missing prior reads as "nothing to protect".
dashboard/data/dashboard_data.json
dashboard/data/insights.json

